<p>
  After an earthquake the ground keeps moving for decades: the rock below the
  plate flows, the stress the earthquake left there relaxes, and a fault that
  was locked creeps. <b>ve</b> shows models of that, solved with
  <a href="https://brendanjmeade.github.io/mbem/">mbem</a>, a mollified
  boundary element method, and <i>without a volume mesh</i>: only the free
  surface, the base of the plate, the faults and the walls of a box carry
  unknowns.
</p>
<p>
  A linear viscoelastic problem is an elastic one at each value of the Laplace
  variable. So a model here is fourteen elastic solves, and every time between
  the earthquake and a hundred relaxation times is one linear combination of
  them.
</p>
